.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt NVME 4
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvme
.Nd polled NVM Express storage driver
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver in the kernel drives one NVM Express controller, found by PCI class
(mass storage, non-volatile memory, NVMe).
With more than one, the first in PCI bus, device, and function order is
used.
It resets and enables the controller with one admin and one I/O queue pair
of 16 entries in kernel memory, identifies the controller and its first
active namespace, and accepts only a namespace formatted with 512-byte
blocks and no metadata; any other format is refused by name.
It moves one 512-byte block per command through a bounce page and polls
for completion with interrupts masked, at most 5000 ticks per command.
A flush is an NVMe FLUSH waited to completion.
.Pp
A namespace is writable only when the boot option
.Ql nvme-write= Ns Ar serial
names its controller's serial number exactly.
Otherwise the driver refuses every write and flush, and init receives a
read-only block handle.
.Pp
At boot the kernel uses an NVMe namespace if a usable controller is
present, and otherwise the ATA disk on the primary IDE channel.
Above the driver nothing knows which is in use.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
On the console:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql NVME CONTROLLER pci=BB:DD.F vendor=... device=... serial=... model=...
the controller was found.
.It Ql NVME NAMESPACE nsid=1 lba_bytes=512 sectors=N access=read-only|read-write
the namespace, and whether this boot may write it.
.It Ql NVME UNAVAILABLE Ar reason
the controller was refused (for example a 4 KiB block format) or failed;
the ATA probe runs instead.
.It Ql BLOCK BACKEND nvme access=...
the block device is this namespace.
.El
.Sh QUALIFICATION
QEMU-qualified on QEMU's NVMe controller model
.Pq Ql -device nvme
with 512-byte blocks, by the kernel's
.Ql make nvme-test
(write, read back, flush, bounds, a fresh boot reading the same data,
read-only without
.Ql nvme-write= ,
refusal of a 4 KiB format) and by the laptop-shaped QEMU machine.
Physical hardware qualification: none.
The physical boot image, whose kernel refuses every disk write, has not yet
been booted on hardware.
.Pp
The qualified SSH boot described in
.Xr afterboot 8
does not use this driver: its disk is QEMU's IDE disk, driven by
.Xr ata 4 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ata 4 ,
.Xr afterboot 8
.Pp
In the kernel repository:
.Pa HANDOFF.md
(Block storage and durable boots) and
.Pa docs/PHYSICAL-BOOT.md .
.Sh CAVEATS
One queue pair, one block per command, polled: throughput is low by
design.
No interrupts, MSI, or namespace other than the first active one.
